                <windows />
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, your house had an older Enviro KHP-2000 Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit. The air intake on the outside of the house was completely covered/blocked with insects and this is quite common for houses located along the river shore in Hay River. The intake usually requires cleaning twice per year, as well as the filters inside the unit. I am unsure as to the current performance of that older unit, and I also could not find accurate data online to describe its' efficiency for fan operation electricity or heat transfer (recovery) efficiency. I used NRCan's default electricity use (with a non-ECM motor) and their default 63% efficiency, although I know some older Fantech units have efficiency below 60% when the temperature outside is below -25C.



The blower door test I performed on your house indicated it is very air tight and it would definitely require an HRV if being built today. Your house relies on the fresh air exchange to ensure good indoor air quality during the heating season.



I recommend that you have the current HRV checked out by a qualified ventilation design contractor (someone who is certified with HRAI) to determine its' current operation &amp; efficiency. If it needs to be replaced, I suggest you upgrade to a new ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, that has an ECM fan motor (not using more than 75 watts) and has a heat transfer efficiency of at least 75%.



Savings: By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV with an ECM motor that is 75% efficient in heat transfer,  you could potentially save $ 76 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.5 GJ. There are currently no rebates for HRV's.</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 151 litre propane hot water tank, that co-vents up the same chimney as the furnace. We talked about an opportunity for you to consider an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92 or higher, could potentially save $ 97 per year in energy cost, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. There are additional savings by eliminating the chimney that co-vents the furnace &amp; hot water tank, however those savings are integrated in with the furnace upgrade section of this report.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older oil furnace (labeled at 82.7% efficiency). We discussed an opportunity for you to upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 2,041 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 40.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



NOTE 1: The savings above assume the house is 100% heated with either the oil furnace or the new propane furnace, without any contribution from the wood stove in the basement. Yes, you use the woodstove to offset heating costs, however not all homeowners will be interested or able to do this, so NRCan has us evaluate &amp; rate houses based on 100% use of the primary heating system (the one where you set the thermostat and let it go).



NOTE 2: There is a heat transfer fan through the basement header space that will blow heat from the woodstove into the garage - we discussed the fact that this opening does not meet code, and that I will not be capturing the heating of the garage in this EnerGuide rating.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time. You have a great opportunity to update the task lighting in your garage with LED !!



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs and fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Wood Stove Supplemental Heat



I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of propane saved from a new high efficiency furnace &amp; a nominal self-harvested cost of wood - see prices outlined a bit lower in this section) if you were to use the wood stove in the basement for about 75% of your basement heating requirements, and it had its' own dedicated outside combustion air supply directly connected to the back.



Savings: By using your wood pellet stove for 75% of the heating requirements of your basement, you could potentially save $ 444 per year in energy costs, in addition to the energy savings previously outlined for installing the 95% efficient propane furnace. If you were to use the ECM motor of that new furnace to circulate the heat throughout the full house, the energy savings would increase significantly.



Of special note(and outlined previously in my report) is that the savings of converting from the older 82.7% efficient oil furnace to the 95% efficient propane furnace are &gt;$2,000. So ........ the incremental savings of offsetting the propane furnace heat with the wood stove become less.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:

        - an Enviro Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV)

        - the kitchen range hood fan

        - 2 bathroom fans

        - the clothes dryer



Overall, the exhaust ventilation of your house (range hood, bathroom fans &amp; dryer) appear to be working well. The HRV is the unit that is older &amp; was completely plugged during my visit. Please see the section of my report on Ventilation regarding a possible upgrade for that HRV. Your house is very air tight and requires an HRV, so you may as well have one that works well, is Energy Star rated, has an ECM fan motor &amp; operates at a high level of heat transfer efficiency.



If you need to upgrade a bathroom fan, consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seal well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test &amp; Combustion Air Supply



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood fan, both bathroom fans and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 10.8 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace, hot water tank and wood stove all vent up chimneys and are definitely susceptible to back drafting. Yes, there is a 6" combustion air supply pipe by the furnace, however I conducted the depressurization test with that 6" pipe fully open and was still able to create 10.8 Pscals of negative pressure (using just your exhaust fans).



The upgrade recommendations to the furnace and hot water would eliminate the chimney for those 2 appliances. I would recommend that you have a dedicated outside combustion air connected to the back of your Blaze King furnace - perhaps re-routing that existing combustion air supply pipe. This should only be completed by a wood stove specialist and in conjunction with the specifications outlined by the Wood Stove Manufacturer.



During my assessment of your home, there were smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.18 per litre 

Propane - $2.42/m3 community piped system, $0.79 per litre delivered

Wood - $289 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $4.73 per bag, $256.46 per tonne bulk delivered



